---
import LinkCard from '../components/ui/LinkCard.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/ui/PageHero.astro';
import Panel from '../components/ui/Panel.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageSeo } from '../seo/page-config';

const seo = pageSeo.verifyOpenpgpFingerprints;
---

<BaseLayout {...seo}>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <div class="page-container-compact">
      <PageHero
        centered={false}
        title="How to Verify OpenPGP Key Fingerprints & Prevent Key Substitution"
        subtitle="Why display names and email addresses inside public keys can be forged, how 40-character hexadecimal fingerprints guarantee identity, and practical verification workflows on macOS."
      />

      <Panel tone="strong" class="p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">The one risk encryption alone cannot solve: Encrypting to an impostor’s key</h2>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            OpenPGP public-key encryption is mathematically virtually unbreakable when used properly. If you encrypt a file to a specific public key, only the corresponding private key can unlock it.
          </p>
          <p>
            However, anyone on the internet can open an OpenPGP tool and generate a brand-new key pair with whatever User ID (Name and Email) they type in—for example, <code>Sarah Chen &lt;sarah@lawfirm-example.com&gt;</code>. If an attacker intercepts an email thread or compromises a web server and swaps Sarah’s real public key file (<code>sarah.asc</code>) with their own fake public key carrying Sarah’s name, you might unwittingly lock your confidential file for the attacker’s key.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Key Fingerprint Verification</strong> closes this loophole completely in less than 30 seconds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">What is an OpenPGP Key Fingerprint?</h2>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Every OpenPGP v4 public key has a unique <strong>160-bit SHA-1 hash</strong> (or 256-bit hash in newer specifications) calculated over the public key’s creation timestamp and mathematical parameters. It is displayed as <strong>40 hexadecimal characters</strong> grouped into ten blocks of four:
          </p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div>3F8B 92C4 D17E 604A 8B11  9C0E 55A2 74DF 19E3 88B6</div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Because this fingerprint is a cryptographic hash of the public key math itself, <strong>no two keys can share the same fingerprint</strong>. Even if an attacker creates a key with the exact same name and email address, the 40-character fingerprint will be completely different.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Three practical ways to verify a contact’s fingerprint out of band</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          “Out-of-band verification” simply means checking the fingerprint over a different communication channel than the one used to send you the `.asc` key file:
        </p>
        <ol class="mt-4 list-inside list-decimal space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Voice or video call read-back (Highest assurance):</strong> If your client or colleague emailed you their public key, import it into Moaiy’s Key Manager and ask them to read the first two and last four groups of their fingerprint during your next Zoom, FaceTime, or phone call.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">End-to-end encrypted messaging (Signal / iMessage / WhatsApp):</strong> If you downloaded a teammate’s public key from GitHub or Slack, ask them to paste their 40-character fingerprint in a verified Signal or iMessage thread.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-text-primary">Cross-checking independent public profiles:</strong> Many security researchers, journalists, and open-source maintainers publish their OpenPGP fingerprint across multiple independent channels—such as their personal domain over HTTPS, their GitHub bio, their X profile, and printed business cards.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">Why you should always check the full 40-character fingerprint (not short 8-char IDs)</h2>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
          <p>
            Legacy PGP tools from the 1990s often displayed only the last 8 hexadecimal characters of a fingerprint (known as the 32-bit “Short Key ID”, such as <code>19E388B6</code>). With modern GPUs, an attacker can brute-force a fake key that matches an 8-character Short Key ID in a matter of seconds (an attack known as a “Deadbeef / Short Key ID collision”).
          </p>
          <p>
            When comparing fingerprints in Moaiy on macOS, always compare the full 40-character fingerprint—or at minimum verify both the leading 16 characters and the trailing 16 characters—so short-ID collision attacks are impossible.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel class="mt-10 p-6 md:p-7">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl font-semibold text-text-primary">How to publish your own fingerprint so collaborators can trust your key</h2>
        <ul class="list-compact mt-3">
          <li>Include your 40-character OpenPGP fingerprint in your email signature alongside a link to your downloadable `.asc` public key.</li>
          <li>Pin your fingerprint in your company’s internal onboarding wiki or GitHub team profile.</li>
          <li>If you rotate or replace your key pair, sign a transition statement with your old key before retiring it so contacts can verify continuity.</li>
        </ul>
      </Panel>

      <section class="link-grid">
        <LinkCard href="/guides/" title="All Guides" description="Browse the full macOS encryption knowledge base." />
        <LinkCard href="/openpgp-made-simple/" title="OpenPGP Made Simple" description="Plain-language guide to public keys and passphrases." />
        <LinkCard href="/cryptographic-signatures-vs-checksums/" title="Signatures vs Checksums" description="How digital signatures prove authorship and integrity." />
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
